  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- A southwesterly system on Sunday will bring additional light snow\n  accumulations.\n\n- A warming and drying trend will arrive by the middle of next\n  week.\n\nA weak transitory ridge of high pressure is expected to slide over\nthe region this afternoon and overnight causing the light shower\nactivity to diminish. This ridge has the potential to allow for\nthe development of low stratus and patchy freezing fog in the\nvalleys. \n\nA warm front is still on track to push across the region on\nSunday. The bulk of the precipitation is expected across north\ncentral Idaho into northwest Montana. The mountain passes along\nthe Montana/Idaho border could experience a couple of inches of\nnew snow causing minor to moderate impacts to the roadways. \n\nA stronger ridge of high pressure builds over the region Monday\nthrough Wednesday. This ridge will cause valley inversions to\ndevelop and strengthen each day. Freezing fog and low stratus \nwill be the biggest impact during this period. The inversions \ncould be strong enough that valleys might experience fog and low \nclouds throughout the day, limiting the potential of reaching the \nmaximum temperatures. \n\nThe longer term models start diverging slightly on their solution\nfor Thanksgiving. Around 60% of the models are indicating that\nwesterly flow will develop over the region with a decent fetch of\nmoisture streaming into the region. This set up will bring a\ndecent amount of snow to the higher terrain and have at least\nmoderate impacts to the mountain passes along the Montana/Idaho\nborder. The other 40% of the models also show the break down of\nthe ridge, but a low pressure digs along the coast dragging the\nbulk of the moisture more over Oregon/California region. \n\nThe models transition back to a ridge of high pressure over the\nNorthern Rockies on Friday into Saturday, but this ridge appears\nto be short lived as the region could become active once again by\nSunday into the following week.",
